  "title": {
    "en": "Skuas, Gannet and Kittiwake"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "The four skuas, and the young seabirds that fly with them"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Skuas are the pirates of the sea. They chase gulls, terns and gannets until they drop their catch. Four species pass the coasts of north-west Europe: Great Skua (bonxie), Arctic, Pomarine and Long-tailed. Which one you see depends on the season: Arctic Skuas are the commonest, Pomarines pass in October and the big autumn storms, and Long-tailed Skuas pass in May and September.\n\nIdentification starts with size and flight: a Great Skua is a heavy, bull-necked brown bird with a white flash, an Arctic is like a dark falcon, a Pomarine is a big, broad, barrel-chested bird, and a Long-tailed is slim and tern-like. Colour varies (pale and dark morphs), and the juveniles are barred and hard. Do not be disappointed if you cannot name each one: the pros leave many as \"skua sp.\".\n\nWith them we take two other birds you see at the same time on a sea-watch: a young Gannet, which is dark brown and speckled, and a juvenile Kittiwake, which has a black W across its wings."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "white flash"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A white patch at the base of the primaries, on the upperwing of a flying skua. Big in Great Skua, small in Arctic."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "spoons"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The blunt, twisted ends of the two central tail feathers of a Pomarine Skua."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "morph"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A colour form within one species. Arctic Skuas occur as pale, intermediate and dark morphs."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "central tail feathers"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The two middle tail feathers. They are pointed in Arctic Skua and very long in an adult Long-tailed Skua."
      }
    }
  ],
